WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.717
A help center had a search box that matched keywords.

00:00:05.017 --> 00:00:07.782
A customer typed: I can't get into my account.

00:00:08.082 --> 00:00:09.516
Zero results.

00:00:10.166 --> 00:00:13.142
The right article was called: how to reset your password.

00:00:13.442 --> 00:00:15.385
Not a single word in common.

00:00:15.685 --> 00:00:16.527
Same topic.

00:00:17.177 --> 00:00:21.762
So the team switched the search to embeddings, and that question started finding the

00:00:21.762 --> 00:00:22.526
right article.

00:00:22.826 --> 00:00:24.943
Two weeks later, a new bug.

00:00:25.243 --> 00:00:30.506
People searching cancel subscription got, as the very first result: how to subscribe

00:00:30.506 --> 00:00:31.696
to the annual plan.

00:00:32.346 --> 00:00:35.012
Similar topic, opposite intent.

00:00:35.312 --> 00:00:40.261
These two cases show exactly what embeddings capture, and where they mislead.

00:00:40.561 --> 00:00:44.944
And by the end of this lesson, you'll know when to trust them, and when to reach for

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:45.727
something else.

00:00:46.927 --> 00:00:52.567
An embedding is a list of numbers, a vector, that represents the meaning of a text.

00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:57.664
An embedding model was trained so that texts with similar meaning become vectors

00:00:57.664 --> 00:00:59.103
that sit close together.

00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:01.118
Picture a huge map.

00:01:01.418 --> 00:01:03.275
Every text gets a point on it.

00:01:03.575 --> 00:01:08.604
Reset password and can't get into my account land on the same street.

00:01:08.904 --> 00:01:12.773
Delivery times by region lands in another neighborhood entirely.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.470
You start with two texts.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.082
A customer's question and a help article, for example.

00:01:19.732 --> 00:01:22.621
The same embedding model processes both.

00:01:23.271 --> 00:01:28.112
Each text becomes a vector, with hundreds or thousands of dimensions.

00:01:28.762 --> 00:01:35.066
A simple calculation, usually cosine similarity, compares the two vectors.

00:01:35.716 --> 00:01:38.463
And the result tells you how close the meanings are.

00:01:38.763 --> 00:01:40.551
The meanings, not the words.

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:43.537
One rule people learn the hard way.

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:47.505
The question and the documents have to go through the same model.

00:01:47.805 --> 00:01:49.860
Each model draws a different map.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.946
Comparing a vector from one model with vectors from another is like comparing a

00:01:54.946 --> 00:01:57.611
street address with a map of another planet.

00:01:57.911 --> 00:02:01.812
So if you change the embedding model, you rebuild the whole index.

00:02:03.012 --> 00:02:05.778
So what does similarity capture well?

00:02:06.078 --> 00:02:07.975
Synonyms and paraphrases.

00:02:08.275 --> 00:02:12.638
Get into my account sits close to log in, and to reset password.

00:02:13.288 --> 00:02:16.725
Different languages, if the model is multilingual.

00:02:17.025 --> 00:02:20.569
Reembolso, in Portuguese, sits close to refund.

00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:22.830
And the general topic.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.546
Texts about the same theme cluster together.

00:02:26.196 --> 00:02:29.196
In Brazilian support, this shows up all the time.

00:02:29.496 --> 00:02:34.158
Pix didn't land, and transfer not credited, share almost no words.

00:02:34.458 --> 00:02:36.127
They name the same pain.

00:02:36.427 --> 00:02:37.734
Embeddings catch that.

00:02:38.034 --> 00:02:41.044
A search that only matches keywords doesn't.

00:02:42.244 --> 00:02:44.025
Now where it misleads.

00:02:44.325 --> 00:02:45.913
Negation and intent.

00:02:46.213 --> 00:02:52.242
Cancel subscription and subscribe to a plan share a topic, and the vector sees the

00:02:52.242 --> 00:02:53.124
topic first.

00:02:53.774 --> 00:02:55.690
Numbers and codes.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:01.301
Order forty-five twenty-one and order forty-five twelve end up almost identical.

00:03:01.601 --> 00:03:04.656
The vector sees: this is an order number.

00:03:04.956 --> 00:03:09.729
For identifiers, tax IDs and tracking codes, filter in the database.

00:03:10.029 --> 00:03:11.296
Not by similarity.

00:03:11.946 --> 00:03:13.490
Rare terms from your domain.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.198
Internal acronyms and product names may be unknown to the model.

00:03:18.848 --> 00:03:20.779
And texts that are too long.

00:03:21.079 --> 00:03:26.428
A twenty page document becomes one single vector, and the meaning of each passage

00:03:26.428 --> 00:03:27.287
gets diluted.

00:03:27.587 --> 00:03:31.849
That's why documents are split into chunks before they become embeddings.

00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:35.778
That last one is the classic beginner trap.

00:03:36.078 --> 00:03:39.799
The team embeds a forty page policy as one vector.

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:45.278
A question about the return window for electronics hits a blob that mixes vacation,

00:03:45.278 --> 00:03:47.648
refunds, returns and a code of ethics.

00:03:47.948 --> 00:03:50.718
The similarity score can even be high.

00:03:51.018 --> 00:03:53.901
But the useful passage is not isolated.

00:03:54.201 --> 00:03:57.421
Chunking exists so each vector stands for one topic.

00:03:58.621 --> 00:04:00.094
Here's one for you.

00:04:00.394 --> 00:04:01.803
Back to the help center.

00:04:02.103 --> 00:04:07.839
A customer searches cancel subscription, and the first result is how to subscribe to

00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:08.931
the annual plan.

00:04:09.231 --> 00:04:10.571
Is the index broken?

00:04:10.871 --> 00:04:13.052
What's the right reading of that ranking?

00:04:16.102 --> 00:04:17.549
The index is fine.

00:04:17.849 --> 00:04:19.348
The embedding did its job.

00:04:19.648 --> 00:04:21.580
It clustered the subscription topic.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.433
Intent just came out inverted.

00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:27.202
To break that tie, you need a signal beyond similarity.

00:04:27.502 --> 00:04:31.223
Keywords, a filter, reranking, or a classifier.

00:04:31.873 --> 00:04:35.982
And swapping for a model that's better in Portuguese won't fix it either.

00:04:36.282 --> 00:04:40.179
The same thing happens in English, with cancel and subscribe.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:42.172
The model might nudge the order.

00:04:42.472 --> 00:04:44.284
The intent limit stays.

00:04:45.484 --> 00:04:48.206
So where do embeddings show up in practice?

00:04:48.506 --> 00:04:51.055
Semantic search.

00:04:51.355 --> 00:04:55.382
Compare the question with chunks of documents, and take the closest.

00:04:56.032 --> 00:04:59.271
Retrieval for generation, or RAG.

00:04:59.571 --> 00:05:03.075
Use that search to choose what goes into the model's context.

00:05:03.375 --> 00:05:04.949
That's the next lesson.

00:05:05.599 --> 00:05:06.293
Clustering.

00:05:06.593 --> 00:05:11.059
Group similar tickets or feedback, without labeling anything first.

00:05:11.709 --> 00:05:12.839
Cheap routing.

00:05:13.139 --> 00:05:15.791
Compare the message with examples of each route.

00:05:16.091 --> 00:05:17.643
But watch for ties.

00:05:17.943 --> 00:05:22.954
A message like I was charged twice for order forty-five twenty-one sits right

00:05:22.954 --> 00:05:24.711
between billing and orders.

00:05:25.011 --> 00:05:30.851
Below a threshold, send it to a classifier or a person, instead of forcing first

00:05:30.851 --> 00:05:31.289
place.

00:05:31.939 --> 00:05:33.737
And semantic cache.

00:05:34.037 --> 00:05:38.067
Reuse the answer to a question that's very close to one you already answered.

00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:40.121
It inherits the same trap.

00:05:40.421 --> 00:05:43.717
How do I cancel sits close to how do I subscribe.

00:05:44.017 --> 00:05:47.398
With a loose threshold, the cache serves the wrong answer.

00:05:47.698 --> 00:05:53.065
So treat the threshold as part of the contract, and test it with opposite intent

00:05:53.065 --> 00:05:53.468
cases.

00:05:54.668 --> 00:06:00.232
On the page there's an experiment that runs on your laptop, no API key, with a small

00:06:00.232 --> 00:06:01.822
open multilingual model.

00:06:02.122 --> 00:06:07.746
You compare one question against four help articles, and print the scores.

00:06:08.396 --> 00:06:13.284
Start with I can't get into my account, and watch reset your password come out on

00:06:13.284 --> 00:06:13.525
top.

00:06:14.175 --> 00:06:19.231
Then change the question to I want to cancel my subscription, and see where the

00:06:19.231 --> 00:06:20.895
annual plan article lands.

00:06:21.195 --> 00:06:26.023
Finally, test order forty-five twenty-one against order forty-five twelve.

00:06:26.323 --> 00:06:30.802
The numbers will show you, on their own, everything this lesson explained.

00:06:31.452 --> 00:06:32.471
And one warning.

00:06:32.771 --> 00:06:35.642
The scores you see belong to this model.

00:06:35.942 --> 00:06:39.787
Don't copy someone else's threshold from the internet as if it were sacred.

00:06:40.087 --> 00:06:41.797
Measure your own pairs.

00:06:42.097 --> 00:06:45.675
The ones that should match, and the ones that must not.

00:06:46.875 --> 00:06:48.990
So what changes in your designs?

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:52.251
Embedding only search is rarely enough.

00:06:52.551 --> 00:06:56.852
Combining it with keyword search is a pattern called hybrid search.

00:06:57.152 --> 00:06:58.941
Remember Pix didn't land?

00:06:59.241 --> 00:07:03.337
With embeddings alone, the right article sat in fifth place.

00:07:03.637 --> 00:07:08.841
Turning on keywords pushed it to first, because Pix is a rare, valuable term your

00:07:08.841 --> 00:07:10.511
domain needs to get right.

00:07:11.161 --> 00:07:15.191
Re-sorting the top results with a model is called reranking.

00:07:15.841 --> 00:07:21.241
How you cut documents into chunks changes search quality as much as the model does.

00:07:21.891 --> 00:07:26.957
And identifiers, dates and amounts get exact filters in the database.

00:07:27.257 --> 00:07:32.417
Pull the order number out of the question, filter by it, and use similarity only on

00:07:32.417 --> 00:07:34.033
the free text that's left.

00:07:35.233 --> 00:07:37.032
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:37.332 --> 00:07:42.645
Embeddings put meaning on a map, which is why they find the password article without

00:07:42.645 --> 00:07:43.974
a single shared word.

00:07:44.274 --> 00:07:47.055
But a map shows closeness, not direction.

00:07:47.355 --> 00:07:51.646
Cancel and subscribe are neighbors, and two order numbers look like twins.

00:07:51.946 --> 00:07:56.283
Use meaning where meaning matters, and exact filters everywhere else.

00:07:56.933 --> 00:08:01.706
In the next lesson, we'll put embeddings to work in the pattern that grounds a model

00:08:01.706 --> 00:08:04.774
in your own documents: retrieval augmented generation.

00:08:05.074 --> 00:08:05.904
See you there.
